Now we will create our personal keys. Public keys will be enrolled into EFI
variables, private keys will be used for signing. Secure Boot depends on the
following keys:

    - Platform Key (PK): an identifier for the computer as a whole; only one PK
        can exist on a system at any time; it authorizes changes to the KEK
        list
    - Key Exchange Key (KEK): verified by the PK; used to authorize updates to
        the database of trusted and forbidden signatures (db and dbx)
    - Signature Database (db): contains trusted certificates, hashes, or
        signatures; when a signed binary is encountered during boot, its
        signature must validate against an entry in the db, or the boot process
        will halt; we will create a custom key whose certificate we will add to
        the database
    - Forbidden Signature Database (dbx): contains revoked or blacklisted
        certificates or hashes; entries here override those in db; if a binary
        matches dbx, it will be blocked even if it is also trusted by db; for
        now, we will leave the dbx empty

We will create our keys using openssl. Get ready for a lot of noise on screen.
